307:
"Āṇattikārakā" means those who carry out the king's orders, remaining after the aforementioned khattiyas who strive in duties. Even if there are gifts to be given (deyyadhamma), if there is no perfection of power (ānubhāva) and perfection of retinue (parivāra), one is unable to give such a gift. In old age, both that power and retinue tend to decline even for such kings; how much more so if there are no gifts to be given? To show this, "deyyadhammasmiñhi," etc., was said. The construction is: "one is unable to give when there are no gifts and in old age." "Etena" means because of the two aforementioned reasons. "Anumatiyā" means by permission. "Pakkhā" means the constituents of the sacrifice along with consideration. Because they prepare and arrange the sacrifice, they are called "parikkhārā" (accessories/requisites). Since they are parts of that sacrifice, they are like a retinue; thus it is said, "parivārā bhavanti" (they become the retinue). The verse starting with "ratho" (chariot) prohibits the unintended meaning in this context:

"The chariot of the Noble Eightfold Path has the white accessories of the Fourfold Purification Virtue;
It has the axle of Jhana associated with Insight (vipassanā), and the wheel of Energy;
It has the pole of Concentration arising from Equanimity;
Non-attachment (alobha) is its covering."

In that verse, "ratho" is the chariot of the Eightfold Path known as the "Divine Vehicle" (brahmayāna). "Setaparikkhāro" means having the ornament of the Fourfold Purification Virtue. There is also a reading "sīlaparikkhāro." "Jhānakkho" means having an axle made of Jhana through the power of the five Jhana factors associated with Insight. "Cakkavīriyo" means having the wheel of Energy. "Upekkhā dhurasamādhi" means having concentration, which is the equality of the two poles/yokes due to equanimity. "Anicchā parivāraṇam" means non-attachment is the covering, like lion skins, etc.

## Description of the Eight Requisites

[They are] those endowed with qualities such as "well-born on both sides" (ubhatosujāta), which will be mentioned. "Yasasā" means by the fivefold power. Therefore, it is said, "because of the ability to establish authority." In the word "saddho" (faithful), "dānassa," etc., was said to show the meaning: "the giver believes that he will experience the fruit of the gift." One who is brave in giving is a "dānasūro" (hero in giving); he is a liberal giver (muttacāga) without even the slightest attachment to the gift. This state is to be understood through the sharpness and clarity of the knowledge of the ownership of deeds (kammassakatā-ñāṇa). To manifest the sharpness and clarity of that knowledge, "saddho" was said first, followed by "dānasūro." Thus it should be understood. Therefore, it was said, "not by mere faith alone," etc. This is said of a person to whom the ownership of deeds appears as if before his eyes. "Yaṃ dānaṃ deti" means whatever gift one gives to another. "Tassa pati hutvā" means thoroughly overcoming greed regarding that object and becoming the master of that object...
